---
import Shot from "../components/Shot.astro";
import ReleaseBlock from "../components/ReleaseBlock.astro";
import StatusBadge from "../components/StatusBadge.astro";
import { appBySlug, repoUrl } from "../data/apps";
import { archiveFolder, assetByKind } from "../data/release-model";
import { releaseFor } from "../data/releases";
import { url } from "../paths";

const app = appBySlug("notes");
const release = releaseFor("notes");
const archive = release ? assetByKind(release, "archive") : undefined;
const repo = repoUrl;
---

<h1 class="app-title">
  {app.icon && <img src={url(app.icon)} width="48" height="48" alt="" />}
  {app.name}
</h1>
<StatusBadge app={app} release={release} />
<p>
  Markdown notes in plain folders. A vault is any folder of Markdown files, so there is
  nothing to import and nothing locked in. Any other Markdown app can open the same notes. It is
  written in Rust, draws with egui, and does not use GTK or Qt. Theme, desktop colors, icons,
  fonts, and in-app windows are the same parts Rivet Files and Rivet FTP use.
</p>
<p>
  {release?.prerelease && "Early alpha. "}
  {release ? (
    <>
      The latest download is <a href={release.htmlUrl}>{release.version}</a>, published {release.dateLabel}.
      {release.prerelease && " GitHub marks it as a pre-release."}
    </>
  ) : (
    <>No release is published yet.</>
  )}
  {release?.prerelease && " It is usable, and still young: things may change or break. Try it on a copy of notes you have backups of."}
</p>


<Shot
  name="notes-live-wide.png"
  alt="Rivet Notes in the pink scheme, live mode, with Peek open"
  caption="Live mode, with Peek open. The line you are on stays Markdown."
  eager
/>

<nav class="onpage" aria-label="On this page">
  <a href="#vaults">Vaults</a>
  <a href="#properties">Properties</a>
  <a href="#browse">Folders and tags</a>
  <a href="#links">Links</a>
  <a href="#editing">Editing</a>
  <a href="#modules">Modules</a>
  <a href="#install">Install</a>
</nav>
<p>How each of these works, and the keys, are in the <a href={url("docs/notes/")}>Notes docs</a>.</p>

<h2 id="vaults">Vaults</h2>
<p>
  A vault is any folder. Its Markdown files are the notes, and its subfolders are their folders.
  Nothing is added to the folder: no database, and no hidden settings. Keep several vaults and
  switch between them.
</p>
<p>
  Notes save as you type. A note changed by another program is reloaded. Deleting a note moves it
  to the desktop's Trash.
</p>

<h2 id="properties">Properties</h2>
<p>
  The frontmatter at the top of a note is shown as properties: text, number, checkbox, date with a
  calendar, list, tags, or link. The file stays plain YAML, and saving writes back only what
  changed.
</p>

<h2 id="browse">Folders and tags</h2>
<p>
  Browse by folder, in a tree that folds, or by tag. The sidebar's panels can be tabs or stacked.
  Search looks at titles, paths, the first line, and tags.
</p>

<h2 id="links">Links</h2>
<p>
  Link notes to each other, to a heading, or with your own shown text. Matches are suggested as
  you type. A link to a note that is not there yet makes it. Backlinks are listed in Peek, and
  renaming a note updates the links that point at it.
</p>

<h2 id="editing">Editing</h2>
<p>
  Two modes. Source mode shows the Markdown as written, colored. Live mode still writes Markdown,
  and renders every line except the one you are on. A reading view shows the whole note rendered,
  with tables, code blocks, and pictures.
</p>
<div class="pair">
  <Shot
    name="notes-source-dark.png"
    alt="Source mode in the pink scheme, showing the Markdown as written"
    caption="Source mode. The same note, as written."
  />
  <Shot
    name="notes-source-wide.png"
    alt="Source mode in the light theme, showing the Markdown as written"
    caption="Source mode, in the light theme."
  />
</div>
<Shot
  name="notes-reading-wide.png"
  alt="The reading view of a note, with a code block and a picture"
  caption="Reading view. Links to other notes open in place."
/>
<p>
  A slash menu adds blocks: headings, lists, todos, quotes, code blocks, dividers, links, and
  today's date. A handle in the margin drags a line, a list item with everything nested under it,
  or a whole code block. Selecting text brings up a small formatting bar.
</p>
<Shot
  name="notes-slash-wide.png"
  alt="The slash menu open in a note, in the light theme"
  caption="The slash menu, in the light theme."
/>
<p>
  Drop files onto a note and they are copied into the vault and linked. Pictures show in place.
  Fonts, text size, and line spacing are yours to set, and the window follows the desktop's
  colors, fonts, and icon theme, in light or dark.
</p>

<h2 id="modules">Modules</h2>
<p>Optional parts you turn on.</p>
<p>
  Daily notes: a note for each day, named by its date, in a folder you pick.
</p>
<p>
  Super todos: tasks with a due date, a priority, and progress, kept as plain text in the note. A
  Todos page lists every one in the vault, as a filtered and sorted list or as a board with To do,
  In progress, and Done columns.
</p>
<div class="pair">
  <Shot
    name="notes-todos-board-wide.png"
    alt="The Todos page as a board, with To do, In progress, and Done columns"
    caption="The Todos page as a board. Drag a card to change its progress."
  />
  <Shot
    name="notes-todos-list-wide.png"
    alt="The Todos page as a list in the light theme, grouped by when each task is due"
    caption="The same page as a list, in the light theme, grouped by due date."
  />
</div>
<Shot
  name="notes-preferences-wide.png"
  alt="Preferences, Modules, with optional modules listed"
  caption="Preferences, Modules. Both modules are optional."
/>

<h2>Quick Open and Quick Actions</h2>
<p>
  Find a note from a few letters of its title or folder, or run any command or option by name.
  They use the same panel as Rivet Files.
</p>
<Shot
  name="notes-quick-open-wide.png"
  alt="Quick Open matching a note from a few letters"
  caption="Quick Open. A few letters of the title find the note."
/>
<p>
  Peek is a panel about the open note: its file, size, word count, reading time, dates, and
  properties.
</p>

<h2 id="install">Install</h2>
<ReleaseBlock slug="notes" />
{
  archive && (
    <>
      <p>
        Unpack the archive and run <code>install.sh</code>. That puts <code>rivet-notes</code> in
        <code>~/.local/bin</code> and adds Rivet Notes to the app launcher.
      </p>
      <pre>
        <code>{`tar xzf ${archive.name}\ncd ${archiveFolder(archive.name)}\n./install.sh`}</code>
      </pre>
    </>
  )
}
<p>
  The program is a portable x86_64 build, linked against glibc 2.31. The window needs
  <a href={url("files/#libraries")}>the same libraries as Rivet Files</a>.
</p>
<h3>From source</h3>
<p>
  Rust 1.88 or newer. From the top of the repository,
  <code>utilities/rivet-dev install notes</code> builds a release and adds the launcher entry. Or:
</p>
<pre><code>cargo build --release -p rivet-notes
./target/release/rivet-notes
./target/release/rivet-notes --install-desktop-entry</code></pre>

<p>
  The docs still list templates, and full-text search in the shared file views, as next. Search
  today looks at titles, paths, the first line, and tags.
</p>
<p>
  Longer notes: <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/rivet-notes.md`}>docs/rivet-notes.md</a>,
  <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/apps/rivet-notes/README.md`}>apps/rivet-notes</a>, and the
  <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/apps/rivet-notes/CHANGELOG.md`}>changelog</a>.
</p>
